# {{ .Name }} ({{ .Type | title }})

Provides a resource to configure the revocation check, with a CRL (certificate revocation list),
of the certificates used for X509 authentication.

## Example Usage

```terraform
# Let the Bastion fetch the CRL from a URL
resource "wallix-bastion_config_x509_crl" "main" {
  crl_check      = true
  crl_auto_fetch = "https://pki.example.com/crl.pem"
}
```

```terraform
# Or upload the CRL
resource "wallix-bastion_config_x509_crl" "main" {
  crl_check = true
  crl_file  = file("${path.root}/crl.pem")
}
```

{{ .SchemaMarkdown | trimspace }}

## Usage Notes

### Singleton Configuration

There is only one CRL configuration on the Bastion and the API has no delete endpoint for it.
Destroying the resource only removes it from the state: the check stays as configured, because
disabling it on destroy (or when the resource is removed from the configuration during a
refactoring) would silently weaken certificate validation. To disable the check, apply
`crl_check = false` before removing the resource.

### CRL Source

`crl_auto_fetch` (the URL the Bastion fetches the CRL from) and `crl_file` (the CRL itself, in PEM
format) are mutually exclusive. `crl_auto_fetch` is sent on every update, empty when it isn't set:
uploading a CRL file doesn't clear the URL on the Bastion, an empty `crl_auto_fetch` does. As a
result, creating the resource on a Bastion whose CRL URL was set elsewhere, for example in the
GUI, clears that URL unless the configuration sets it: import the existing configuration first.

With `crl_auto_fetch`, the Bastion downloads the CRL while the configuration is applied, and the
apply fails if the download fails. The URL must be resolvable and reachable from the Bastion, not
from the host running Terraform. If the network of the Bastion drops the connection instead of
rejecting it, the apply waits until the Bastion gives up, or until the default timeout of
Terraform operations (20 minutes).

A CRL is only valid until its `nextUpdate` date: with `crl_file`, plan to apply a new one before
that date, typically from a scheduled pipeline.

### `crl_file` Is Write-Only

The API never returns the CRL file. Terraform can't detect a change made to it outside of
Terraform, and an imported resource has no `crl_file` in state: if it is set in the configuration,
the next apply uploads it again.

The file is only uploaded on creation and when `crl_file` changes in the configuration. A CRL
uploaded outside of Terraform, for example an emergency revocation from the GUI, is kept by updates
that don't change `crl_file`, and replaced the next time it changes.

Removing `crl_file` from the configuration doesn't remove the uploaded CRL from the Bastion: the
provider then sends nothing for the file, and the API has no endpoint to delete it.

If an upload fails, the previous file stays in state, so the next plan proposes the new file again.

## Import

The CRL configuration can be imported using any ID (it is always stored as `x509CRLConfig` in
state, since the API doesn't provide one), e.g.

```shell
terraform import wallix-bastion_config_x509_crl.main x509CRLConfig
```
